---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import Cta from '../components/home/Cta.astro';

const grants: [string, string][] = [
  ["The task's worktree", 'Read and write'],
  ['The repository\'s .git', 'Read-only, except objects, refs and logs, so commits land but hooks and config cannot change'],
  ['A package cache for the project', 'Read and write, shared by the project\'s tasks, outside every worktree'],
  ['Ouijit\'s status port', 'Reachable, so the card still shows what the agent is doing'],
  ['Your home directory, keys, dotfiles and other repositories', 'Blocked'],
  ['The network', 'Allowed by default. Block it, or open ports, in the project\'s profile.'],
];
---
<Site
  title="Sandbox · Ouijit"
  description="Run coding agents with their permission checks off, held to their task's worktree by kernel-level limits, while the dev server beside them stays on the host.">
  <main class="compare-content">
    <h1>Permission checks off, machine intact</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Every agent CLI has a flag that stops it asking before each command. It is the fastest way to work and the
      most dangerous, because the agent then has everything you have. Ouijit's sandbox holds that agent to its
      task's worktree.
    </p>
    <img
      class="compare-screenshot"
      src="/assets/screenshots/settings.png"
      alt="Project settings with a start hook that runs Claude Code with permission checks off, in the sandbox"
      loading="lazy"
    />

    <h2>What the agent can reach</h2>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        {grants.map(([a, b]) => (
          <tr><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The limits are enforced by the kernel, with Seatbelt on macOS and Landlock on Linux, through
      <a href="https://github.com/nolabs-ai/nono" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nono</a>. The agent runs in
      place, in the real worktree, as a normal process. There is no virtual machine to boot and no files to sync,
      so a sandboxed terminal opens as fast as a plain one, and the agent's own sign-ins work.
    </p>

    <h2>The agent is sandboxed, the dev server isn't</h2>
    <p>
      A project picks a default, Host or Sandbox, and anything can go elsewhere: a terminal, one hook, one run of a
      hook, a script, or a single command in a longer line. The usual shape is an agent in the sandbox with the dev
      server and the tests beside it on the host, and a done hook that pushes from the host because the host has
      the credentials.
    </p>
    <pre><code>git pull origin main &amp;&amp; ouijit sandbox -- claude --dangerously-skip-permissions "$OUIJIT_TASK_DESCRIPTION"</code></pre>
    <p>
      That start hook pulls on the host and runs only the agent inside the sandbox. A sandboxed terminal wears an
      outlined status dot so you can tell at a glance.
    </p>

    <h2>Still an agent in Ouijit</h2>
    <p>
      A sandboxed agent keeps the <a href="/docs/cli/"><code>ouijit</code> command</a>, with a token of its own
      scoped to its project. By default it can read its task, read the notes you leave on its diff, draft review
      comments for you to send, and ask you to run a command it cannot. You can widen that to moving its own task,
      or to everything, per project. Comments it drafts are marked as written from a sandbox.
    </p>

    <h2>Fails closed</h2>
    <p>
      If the sandbox cannot start, the terminal says why and the command does not run. Nothing ever falls back to
      the host. The profile that defines the limits lives outside the repository, where the agent cannot edit it.
    </p>

    <h2>Or bring your own</h2>
    <p>
      Teams with their own sandboxing can set a Custom launcher: a reviewed script that owns the boundary, with
      Ouijit passing the worktree, git directories and ports as hints. The <a href="/docs/sandbox/">sandbox
      documentation</a> has the contract.
    </p>
  </main>
  <Cta />
</Site>
